  "worked_solution": "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but this is already excluded. \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-3,1)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(-5,0)$ contain the excluded point $x=-3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(1,5)$ and $(0,\\infty )$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=4$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
